==Taxonomy==
===Classification===
The ''Bulbinella'' [[genus]] was first established in 1843 by [[Carl Sigismund Kunth]].{{sfn|Moore|1964|p=6}}{{Sfn|Perry|1987|p=1}} Initially, six species from the [[Cape of Good Hope]], in South Africa, were placed in the genus ''Bulbinella''; three of them transferred from other genera and three of the species were described as new. In 1845, [[Joseph Dalton Hooker]] based his new genus ''Chrysobactron'' on ''B. rossii'' specimens collected from [[Campbell Island, New Zealand|Campbell Island]]. The species was first published in the ''[[Flora Antarctica]]'' by him, noting that ''Chrysobactron'' was "very nearly allied" and had a similar appearance to the South African ''Bulbinella'' genus, but he decided not to move the new genus into it.{{Sfn|Moore|1964|p=2}}{{Sfn|Hooker|1859|p=73}}

In 1906, botanist [[Thomas Cheeseman]] had more than once expressed doubts about the generic status of the New Zealand species and placed them in ''Bulbinella'' rather than in ''Chrysobactron''.{{Sfn|Cheeseman|1906|p=718}} In 1952, [[Lucy Cranwell]] studied the pollination of various New Zealand plant species; in her study, she mentioned the points of similarity between the grains of South African ''Bulbinella'' species and New Zealand ''Chrysobactron'' (now known as ''Bulbinella'') species, but no other differences were mentioned by her.{{Sfn|Moore|1964|pp=1–2}}{{Sfn|Cranwell|1952|p=50}}

There are twenty-three species in the genus ''Bulbinella''; seventeen of which are located in South Africa, and six in New Zealand.{{sfn|Musara|Spies|Spies|Stedje|2017|pp=1–2}} A 2017 thesis by Collen Musara examined the genus ''Bulbinella'' and hypothesised that ''B. rossii'' is the closest resemblance to the South African species because of ''B. rossii''{{'}}s fibrous leaf bases that other New Zealand ''Bulbinella'' species do not possess.{{Sfn|Musara|2017|p=229}} ''B. rossii'' is closely related to ''B. gibbsii'', but differs due to its taller stature, broader leaves (up to 60 mm wide), and cylindric racemes supported on a stout axis that is hidden by dense flowers and fruits.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of ''B. rossii''{{'}}s genus name, ''Bulbinella'', derives from the [[Greek language|Greek]] {{lang|el|βολβός}}, which is a name for a [[bulb]], an underground plant storage structure. Both words ''Bulbine'' and ''Bulbinella'' translate to English as 'little bulb'.{{sfn|Gledhill|2008|p=90}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name) ''rossii'' is named after [[James Clark Ross|James Ross]], the leader of Hooker's expedition. Hooker decided to name the specific epithet in honour of Ross because he brought many new species to him during their short stay on Campbell Island. The species is [[Common name|commonly known]] as the Ross lily.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{Sfn|Hooker|1859|p=73}}

==Distribution==
[[File:Ross Lily (Bulbinella rossii) on Enderby Island.png|thumb|right|''B. rossii'' on [[Enderby Island]]|alt=A tussock grassland with a population of Bulbinella rossii individuals in the foreground of the image.]]
''Bulbinella'' species are predominantly [[endemism|endemic]] to South Africa, but six species are endemic to New Zealand, most of which are found predominantly in the [[South Island]].{{sfn|Richardson|Lord|Perry|2017|p=1}} ''B. rossii'' is endemic to New Zealand's subantarctic [[Auckland Islands]] and [[Campbell Islands]].{{sfn|Musara|Spies|Spies|Stedje|2017|p=5}} There, it is common and widespread from sea level to the highest parts of the island's mountains. Because it thrives where the ground has been disturbed, and because it is not particularly of interest to browsing animals, it is common near former human habitation sites, and may form dense colonies in open [[herbfield]]s and [[tussock grassland]]s.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}

In 1970, botanist R. H. Taylor studied the vegetation and wildlife of [[Enderby Island]]. In his study, he noted that ''B. rossii'' appeared to be more common on the north and west sides of the island, while the south-east sides of the island appeared to be dominated by [[southern rātā]] (''Metrosideros umbellata'') forests.{{sfn|Taylor|1971|pp=11–12, 22}}

===Habitat===

All New Zealand ''Bulbinella'' species are [[allopatric]], prefer colder habitats and soils with high water content.{{sfn|Musara|Spies|Spies|Stedje|2017|p=2}}{{Sfn|Perry|1999|p=9}} On the main [[Auckland Island]], ''B. rossii'' occurs from sea level to high-altitude areas; but it is uncommon at lower-altitudes and is more common at higher-altitude areas where the ground is unfavourable for ''B. rossii'' to be rooted by introduced animals.{{Sfn|Johnson|Campbell|1975|p=22}}{{sfn|McGlone|Moar|1997|p=4}} On Campbell Island, ''B. rossii'' only becomes predominant above {{Convert|250–300|m|abbr=on}} above sea level.{{sfn|McGlone|Wilmshurst|Richardson|Turney|2019|p=3}}

==Ecology==
{{quote box|There is a weed which they call 'Bulbinella Rosi' {{Sic}} growing on the island, which is spreading very rapidly, and appears to be going to over-run the country. The paddocks are simply a mass of it and where it is growing very thick, no other vegetation will thrive.|—M. McKay, 1910|width=25em |align=right}}
The roots of ''B. rossii'' are often eaten by pigs, but the species is avoided by cattle and goats.{{Sfn|Johnson|Campbell|1975|p=22}}{{sfn|McGlone|Moar|1997|p=4}} ''B. rossii''{{'}}s bitter taste also makes it unattractive to sheep.{{Sfn|Moore|1964|p=6}} On Campbell Island in 1910, M. McKay, a stock inspector who was on the island to inform New Zealand's [[Minister of Agriculture (New Zealand)|Ministry of Agriculture]] about the cases of sheep scab on the island, mentioned that ''B. rossii'' was so common on the island that it was likened to an [[invasive species]].{{sfn|Horton Hartley|Beattie|Lord|2022|p=19}}

A 2013 research article by the ''[[New Zealand Journal of Botany]]'' reported on the floral biology and pollination on Campbell Island. In it, the researchers observed many of Campbell Island [[wētā]] (''Notoplectron campbellensis'') individuals feeding on the pollen and nectar of ''B. rossii'', with as many as eight individuals on an inflorescence.{{sfn|Lord|Huggins|Little|Tomlinson|2013|p=176}}{{sfn|Pattemore|2013|p=2}} ''B. rossii'' was the most frequently visited species from their study. Other species noted in their study included: ''[[Melangyna novaezelandiae]]'' and ''[[Scoparia parmifera]]''.{{sfn|Lord|Huggins|Little|Tomlinson|2013|pp=174-176}} ''B. rossii'' individuals are also known to be visited by [[Hoverfly|hoverflies]] and other small flies.{{sfn|Buxton|Anderson|Hoare|Lord|2019|p=10}}{{sfn|Godley|1982|p=12}}

A 2019 study conducted by ''[[Polar Research]]'' examinined the moths on New Zealand's subantarctic islands. Researchers noted that ''[[Ichneutica erebia]]'', an endemic [[moth]], was observed to visit the inflorescences of ''B. rossii'' and may be an important pollinator for the species. ''I. erebia'' moths either seek ''B. rossii'' or are capable of travelling long distances at night and have more active foraging.{{sfn|Buxton|Anderson|Hoare|Lord|2019|pp=7–8}} The seeds of ''B. rossii'' are [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by the wind.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}

==Conservation==
''Bulbinella rossii'' was evaluated in the most recent 2024 assessment by the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System|New Zealand Threatened Classification System]] as "At Risk — Naturally Uncommon" because its distribution is confined to the Auckland and Campbell Islands groups.{{Sfn|New Zealand Threat Classification System|2024}} ''B. rossii'' appears to have no threats, and is listed because of its confined geographical area to the Auckland and Campbell Islands.{{sfn|De Lange|Gosden|Courtney|Fergus|2024|pp=54–55}}{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}

==Recognition==
{{As of|2025}}, ''Bulbinella rossii'' is featured on the reverse of the [[New Zealand five-dollar note]].{{Sfn|Reserve Bank of New Zealand|2024}}

